on.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e713c421-2b00-5045-b24a-0f7fbd12429c" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-112434" :question/problem "Given that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x)=7{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+x-1,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 1 & 7 & -7 & 1 & -1 \\\\  &  & 7 & 0 & 1 \\\\  & 7 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x-1}=7{x}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-1)(7{x}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $7{x}^{2}+1=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(0{)}^{2}-4(7)(1)=-28<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7(0{)}^{3}-7(0{)}^{2}+0-1=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-1),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=1,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-112434-a-1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde30544-dd02-5df3-838f-896d81562ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f852022-e1f7-5543-971f-4e1505078600" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-28197-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248596} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf54fe6-22f2-5727-b96a-a2828da7f313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-28197-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248597} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c45b64-5090-5a18-9e7d-13f38da33b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-28197-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248598} {:answer/id #uuid "b832742c-4a0b-514d-a4b4-44d15e07c08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-28197-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248599} {:answer/id #uuid "a4cdeb7f-ecec-5f1b-bea4-6e92b69b31ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-28197-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0c45b64-5090-5a18-9e7d-13f38da33b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-28197-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248598} :answer-field/id #uuid "4717dd12-e65c-550b-8dc4-1967aa72efff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248595}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24fcd9b1-0c96-5914-b19d-38120c8b01d7" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-28197" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}(4-x)({x}^{2}+6)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+6=0$ is \n\n$$\nD={0}^{2}-4(1)(6)=-24<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=4.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{8}(4-0)({0}^{2}+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4)(6)}{8} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-28197-e-0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ef84d8b-a825-581a-aa43-2b18b42ab882" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "966f35c2-66c5-5091-abf5-0be88c14a7cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39032-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248603} {:answer/id #uuid "7a813be0-03f1-510f-9e94-caacd47988a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39032-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248604} {:answer/id #uuid "8a13d929-24ae-58fb-bacb-94cc50f1ff7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39032-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248605} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ac82d8-dc15-51eb-a2ff-45ed87b9ab28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39032-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248606} {:answer/id #uuid "4deab048-da77-52b3-a408-72371f192a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39032-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4deab048-da77-52b3-a408-72371f192a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39032-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248607} :answer-field/id #uuid "e67a7594-2bd2-5bc7-bb9c-21f2a461d7a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248602}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "438b67c5-d731-547c-860c-c6e1368047db" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-39032" :question/problem "Given that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+5x-6,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 & 5 & -6 \\\\  &  & 2 & -2 & 6 \\\\  & 1 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x-2}={x}^{2}-x+3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-2)({x}^{2}-x+3).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}-x+3=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(1{)}^{2}-4(1)(3)=-11<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=2.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={0}^{3}-3(0{)}^{2}+5(0)-6=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-6),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39032-a-1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186248608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fd988de-d8c3-5dcb-b3c3-636d4173d5de" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256ef07d-64c2-536e-a16e-695f42458eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39091-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248610} {:answer/id #uuid "1173463a-af3f-5425-b9df-f86429cb5c72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39091-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248611} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e7ea27-a50c-5200-8fc8-5e06308d3c5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39091-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248612} {:answer/id #uuid "2018cfe9-d083-531e-8aad-0955d336d71c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39091-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248613} {:answer/id #uuid "26e8ad73-5b7f-537d-83e9-d29d926bce89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/review-13925710/assets/q-39091-a-3.png" :db/id 